---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

// 12 October 2026 (XC): B5 goes on the site as a project introduction only — no manuscript,
// no results. The manuscript is in preparation; numbers stay in the Vault until XC has read it.
const project = projects.modifiers;
---

<Base
  title={`${project.title} · Xiaochuan Li`}
  description="Which property answers shift in language models when a modifier adds a feature to a noun? Three accounts compared with predictors fixed outside the models. Manuscript in preparation."
  showProgress={true}
  toc={true}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/research#research" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Research & Projects</a></p>
    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-2">{project.status}</div>
    <h1>{project.title}</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] italic !text-lg !mb-3">{project.question}</p>
    <p>{project.summary}</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="/images/research/modified-nouns-measure.png" width="913" height="913" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="Schematic of the measure: the prompt It was a winged dog. Question: Can it fly?, scored as log P(Yes) minus log P(No); on a score axis, a winged dog is compared with an old dog (common modifiers, DiD) and with an aquatic dog (cross-group control, DiD×)." />
      <figcaption>How a shift is measured: the same yes/no question after the target phrase and after matched controls. Marker positions are schematic, not data.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Three accounts</h2>
    <p>
      A modified noun rarely keeps all the properties of its head: a <em>wooden spoon</em> keeps the
      function of a spoon but not its metal look. Telling someone that a dog has wings sets one feature.
      On a causal-model view of concepts, properties the feature causes or enables (<em>it can fly</em>)
      should follow, while properties that are only typical of winged things (<em>it has a beak</em>)
      should not. On a category-based view, the modifier is evidence about what kind of thing is
      described, so bird properties rise together. On an associative view, the word <em>winged</em>
      simply primes related words.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each account gets an item-level predictor that does not come from the models: a dependency table
      written from theory and fixed before testing, feature co-occurrence in human property norms, and
      word-association strength in human association norms. On these items the three predictors come
      apart, so they can be compared within one analysis.
    </p>

    <h2>Materials and controls</h2>
    <p>
      Thirteen feature groups (<em>winged</em>, <em>glass</em>, <em>motorized</em>, …) are applied to
      nouns that lack the feature. For each phrase the model is asked about dependent properties,
      co-occurring properties and the noun’s own characteristic property. Every item is also asked about
      the bare noun, about the noun with common modifiers (<em>old</em>, <em>nearby</em>), and about the
      noun with a feature from another group, which is just as unusual but unrelated to the property in
      question. A separate set removes features (<em>wingless</em>).
    </p>

    <h2>Models and measures</h2>
    <p>
      Three instruction-tuned models answer yes/no questions; the score is the log-odds of <em>Yes</em>
      over <em>No</em> at the first answer token, checked under two further phrasings. Before any
      modifier effect is read, bare-noun scores must agree with human norms. Confidence intervals
      resample feature groups, so the dependency relations, not the phrases, are the unit of
      generalization. The success criteria were fixed before the runs.
    </p>

    <h2>Inside the model</h2>
    <p>
      An exploratory activation-patching analysis on pilot materials copies the residual stream at the
      modifier or noun position into a matched phrase, one layer at a time, to see where the shift
      arises. It is a first look, not yet a full analysis.
    </p>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p class="cw-status">
      Manuscript in preparation.
    </p>
    <p class="!mt-10 !text-sm !text-[var(--color-text-dim)]"><a href="/research">← Back to Research & Projects</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>
